mallocng: representable object bounds for the virtual profile (option iii)

Three changes, each necessary and none sufficient alone. Seven size-class strides raised by
1-4 units so every large class's stride is a multiple of its own bounds granule; a
granule-sized group header so the first slot's base is a multiple of it too; and enframe
stepping each object's base DOWN to a granule-aligned offset within its slot.

Without them the compressed encoder truncates an unaligned base DOWNWARD and the object
gains authority below itself. Measured against the RTL bounds model over 6060
(class, request, slot, offset-cycle) cases: 1497 decode wrong with the step-down alone and
a 16-byte header -- the first being class 36 at n=16480, slot 0, offset 0, whose base
0x400000010 decodes as [0x400000000, 0x400004080), sixteen bytes below the object -- and 0
with all three in place.

The header costs nothing. alloc_group already rounds the group UP to a page, and over every
class and every reachable cnt that rounding leaves at least the pad; the margin is exact in
places (class 36 at cnt 7 has 16 bytes for a 16-byte pad), so cap_hdr_fits asserts it and a
future table or count change fails loudly instead of overrunning the group.

The step-down is down and never up, so it cannot exceed slack, and it is a no-op for any
granule at or below UNIT -- every length below 8 KiB -- so the double-free offset cycling is
untouched there and keeps 1 offset in granule/UNIT above it.

size_to_class and the slot/class selection are untouched: the request is rounded in the
runtime's own malloc wrapper, outside musl, so set_size records exactly what the capability
covers. check-mallocng-policy.py pins the seven entries and their values and still refuses
any other difference.

